# Wire read

Total readers of JSON fields, called after a shape check has established what
they read, so none of them can fail: an absent or wrongly typed field reads as
the empty value of its type. The one strict door in is `parseStrict`, which
refuses a duplicated key instead of keeping either value silently.

## Note
The text under `key`, or the empty text.

## To textAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Text:
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JStr (s):
            Return s.
        Otherwise:
            Return "".
    Return "".

## Note
The integer under `key`, or `0`.

## To intAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Int:
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JNum (n):
            Return n.
        Otherwise:
            Return 0.
    Return 0.

## Note
The boolean under `key`, or `false`.

## To boolAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Bool:
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JBool (b):
            Return b.
        Otherwise:
            Return false.
    Return false.

## Note
The strings of the array under `key`, in order, other elements skipped.

## To textsAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Seq of Text:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JArr (elems):
            Repeat for e in elems:
                Inspect e:
                    When JStr (s):
                        Push s to out.
                    Otherwise:
                        Let ignored be e.
        Otherwise:
            Return out.
    Return out.

## Note
The text under `key` when it is a string, `none` when it is `null` or absent: a
missing value is kept apart from an empty one.

## To optTextAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Option of Text:
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JStr (s):
            Return some s.
        Otherwise:
            Return none.
    Return none.

## Note
The value at a dotted path through nested objects, or `JNull`.

## To jsonPath (v: Json, path: Text) -> Json:
    Let mutable here be v.
    Repeat for step in split(path, "."):
        Set here to jsonGet(here, step).
    Return here.

## Note
A document parsed strictly, as a `Result` that composes with the rest of a decoder.

## To parseStrict (text: Text) -> Result of Json and Text:
    Inspect jsonParseStrict(text):
        When JOk (v):
            Return a new Ok with value v.
        When JErr (m):
            Return a new Err with error m.
    Return a new Err with error "unparsed".

## Test: total readers read what is there and the empty value otherwise

Let v be jsonParse("{{\"a\": \"x\", \"n\": -4, \"ok\": true, \"tags\": [\"p\", 7, \"q\"], \"m\": null}}").
Expect that textAt(v, "a") is equal to "x".
Expect that intAt(v, "n") is equal to -4.
Expect that boolAt(v, "ok") is equal to true.
Expect that textsAt(v, "tags") is equal to ["p", "q"].
Expect that optTextAt(v, "m") is none.
Expect that optTextAt(v, "a") is equal to some "x".
Expect that textAt(v, "missing") is equal to "".
Expect that intAt(v, "a") is equal to 0.

## Test: a dotted path walks nested objects

Let v be jsonParse("{{\"t\": {{\"p\": {{\"low\": 0.4}}}}}}").
Expect that jsonSerialize(jsonPath(v, "t.p.low")) is equal to "0.4".
Expect that jsonIsNull(jsonPath(v, "t.q.low")) is equal to true.

## Test: a strict parse refuses a duplicated key

Inspect parseStrict("{{\"a\": 1, \"a\": 2}}"):
    When Ok (v):
        Expect that jsonSerialize(v) is equal to "refused".
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e contains "duplicate".

## Test: a boolean field reads back as itself

For all booleans b:
    Let v be a new JObj with entries [a new JEntry with key "ok" and val (a new JBool with b b)].
    Expect that boolAt(v, "ok") is equal to b.
